  {
    "id": "alfarabi_950g",
    "titleEnglish": "First Oud Tuning (Full First Octave) 27-Tone",
    "titleArabic": "تنغيم العود الأول (الديوان الأول الكامل) 27-نغمة",
    "year": "950g",
    "sourceEnglish": "",
    "sourceArabic": "",
    "sourcePageReferences": [
      {
        "sourceId": "farmer_1937",
        "page": "249"
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "land_1885",
        "page": "134-137"
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "derlanger_1930",
        "page": "165-185"
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "forster_2010",
        "page": "639"
      }
    ],
    "creatorEnglish": "al-Fārābī",
    "creatorArabic": "الفارابي",
    "commentsEnglish": "This is al-Fārābī's first oud tuning system, created by placing frets to divide four oud strings. Although rendered in most musicological publications as a tuning system of 10-frets and 25-tones per octave (Farmer 1937:249 renders it as 9-frets and 21-tones), it doesn't include second octave equivalents for all the notes in the first octave - a fact explicitly stated by al-Fārābī himself (see original Arabic in Land 1855:139). For this reason al-Fārābī adds a theoretical fifth string (ḥādd) in order to rectify the problem and render his second oud tuning of 22-Tones which includes double octaves.\n\nAdditionally, for the first oud tuning, al-Fārābī explicitly states that his placement of the frets renders 10 unique notes per string using 9-frets (Land 1855:136), but ultimately gives 11-fret divisions including an alternative placement for the 27/22 wuṣta zalzal fret as 19683/16384 labelled lj (لج) whilst explicitly stating that not all the frets are used at any one time. Only one of the two frets labelled kd (كد) at 162/149 and kḥ (كح) at 54/49 are used, and only one of the two frets labelled th (ث) at 81/68 and ẓ (ظ) at 27/22 should be used (or its alternative lj (لج) at 19683/16384). The full list of divisions produced from the 11-frets gives us 27-tones in the first octave.\n\nFor this reason, some tuning system values do not have note names mapped to them because they would have to be doubled such as 54/49 which should also be named ʿirāq due to al-Fārābī giving the two values 162/149 and 54/49 as either/or.\n\nThey are all included because that is how they are published in the relevant sources.",
    "commentsArabic": "",
    "tuningSystemPitchClasses": [
      "1/1",
      "256/243",
      "18/17",
