  {
    "id": "ibnmunajjim_912",
    "titleEnglish": "10-Tone",
    "titleArabic": "10-نغمات",
    "year": "912",
    "sourceEnglish": "",
    "sourceArabic": "",
    "sourcePageReferences": [
      {
        "sourceId": "wright_1966",
        "page": "28"
      }
    ],
    "creatorEnglish": "Ibn Munajjim",
    "creatorArabic": "إبن منجّم",
    "commentsEnglish": "Although Wright states that Ibn Munajjim \"fails to supply any definition of tunings and intervals\", he goes on to say that \"there is a passage in which he explains that the notes on the two lower strings may be disregarded because the two upper strings produce the same notes at the octave\" (1966:27). Wright then goes on to schematize Ibn Munajjim's abjad based octave equivalences and deduces the tuning in cents rather than fraction ratios, based on the assumption that the four open strings are tuned in fourths as follows: 0, 90, 204, 294, 498, 588, 702, 792, 906, 996 (1966:28).\n\nAs these cents values align perfectly with well-known and often used ratios for tuning systems of the same historical period, they are provided here as fraction ratios instead of the cents provided by Wright.",
    "commentsArabic": "",
    "tuningSystemPitchClasses": [
