  {
    "id": "mukhtarandmushrrafa_1944",
    "titleEnglish": "Egyptian Tuning 22-Tone",
    "titleArabic": "التنغيم المصري 22 نغمة",
    "year": "1944",
    "sourceEnglish": "",
    "sourceArabic": "",
    "sourcePageReferences": [
      {
        "sourceId": "shawqi_1969",
        "page": "139"
      }
    ],
    "creatorEnglish": "Mukhtār and Mushrrafa",
    "creatorArabic": "مشرفة ومختار",
    "commentsEnglish": "According to Shawqī (1969:135), quoting from Mukhtār (1944), the research project began in 1935. The researchers Alī Muṣṭafā Mushrrafa and Maḥmūd Mukhtār asked the musicians Muṣṭafā Riḍā, Muṣṭafā al-'Aqqād and Ibrahīm Farūz to each tune their own Qanūn and prepare it for the performance of a specific maqām, and then oversee the recording and tuning analysis of each note.\n\nThe values in Shawqī are given as a table with multiple columns that is confusing. The tables includes measurements aligning with a partial list of the note names in Arabic (14 note names) because \"the tests were undertook on a limited selection of maqāmāt including maqāmāt iraq, ḥijāz, nahāwand, ṣabā and ḥijāz kār and therefore only the note names from those maqāmāt were used\". These are presented alongside another column that includes a full list of 23 note names in solfege alongside their measurements. The confusion arises because some notes have two different values in columns marked as Results of the Experiment, and Full List, something which Shawqī does not address.\n\nThe version saved here takes the 14 measurements from the Arabic note names list (Results of the Experiment column), and the remaining rest from the Full List of 23.\n\nThe original values reprinted by Shawqī are given as frequencies in Hz starting from 1000, as listed below.\n\nOriginal values in Hz:\n1000, 1057, 1080, 1123, 1171, 1190, 1228, 1274, 1302, 1330, 1417, 1440, 1498, 1562, 1590, 1685, 1738, 1779, 1831, 1880, 1920, 1955, 2000.\n\nWe have converted them to cents in order to enter them accurately in this database. The cents conversion can be verified by changing the Frequency value of the first pitch class to 1000 Hz.\n\nWe have been unable to source a copy of Mukhtār (1944), but we do have access to Mokhtar and Mosharrafa (1937) which is where the original 14 measurements were published, and listed as decimal ratios. We have included it in our database, in the original decimals ratios values, under Mokhtar and Mosharrafa (1937) because it is an English language article and that's how their names are spelled.",
    "commentsArabic": "",
    "tuningSystemPitchClasses": [
      "0.000",
