  {
    "id": "alsabbagh_1950",
    "titleEnglish": "Contemporary Arabic 19-Tone",
    "titleArabic": "السلم العربي الحالي",
    "year": "1950",
    "sourceEnglish": "",
    "sourceArabic": "",
    "sourcePageReferences": [
      {
        "sourceId": "shawqi_1969",
        "page": "160"
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "alsabbagh_1950",
        "page": "173"
      }
    ],
    "creatorEnglish": "al-Ṣabbāgh",
    "creatorArabic": "الصبّاغ، توفيق",
    "commentsEnglish": "This tuning system by Tawfīq al-Ṣabbāgh is built on a theoretical 53-comma per octave division, resulting in 19-tones that are used in the maqāmāt he recounts in his book. Ṣabbāgh gives the comma a value of 1/81th of a string length of 10,000 = 123.4567901235 (1950:38, defined in error by Shawqī as 0,012346 in 1969:147). This is a syntonic comma, also known as the Didymus' comma, the meantone comma or the Ptolemaic comma, with a frequency ratio of 81/80. al-Ṣabbāgh proceeds to calculate the notes of many maqāmāt based on the principle distances between the fundamental notes as follows:\n\nrāst (9 commas) dūgāh (7) segāh (6) chahārgāh (9) nawā (9) ḥusaynī (7) awj (6) kurdān\n\nThe final 19-tone system is then given as as string lengths and frequency in Hz starting from rāst at 10000 and 516 Hz plus the number of commas per interval between the 19-tones (which are either 5,4,3,2 or 1 commas).\n\nHere we have set the default frequency at 258 Hz for rast (an octave lower than Ṣabbāgh) but the frequency of rāst-10000-1/1 as 130.37 to match other tuning systems in the database for the sake of clarity and comparison.\n\nṢabbāgh also uses the note names kurdī wāṭī and kurdī ʿālī instead of nīm kurdī and kurdī, nīm būseḹīk instead of būselīk, ḥiṣār wāṭī and ḥiṣār ʿālī instead of nīm ḥiṣār and ḥiṣār, and nīm māhūr wāṭī and nīm māhūr ʿālī instead of nīm māhūr and tīk mahūr.",
    "commentsArabic": "",
    "tuningSystemPitchClasses": [
